<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>app.curant.facetime</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <!-- Deliberately NOT /usr/bin/python3 (Apple's system Python).
             macOS's Accessibility/Screen Recording grants (System
             Settings → Privacy & Security) are tied to the exact
             executable path that requests them, not to whichever
             terminal/parent launched it. Confirmed live: running via
             /usr/bin/python3 under launchd hit "Accessibility
             privileges not enabled" and "could not create image from
             display" even though the SAME script running under
             Homebrew's python3 in a foreground Terminal worked fine —
             because that's a genuinely different binary that had never
             been granted anything. This path matches sys.executable
             from a working foreground run (`python3 -c "import sys;
             print(sys.executable)"`), reusing the grants already given
             to it. If you reinstall/upgrade python@3.12 via Homebrew
             and this path changes, re-run that command and update this
             plist to match, and re-grant Accessibility/Screen Recording
             to the new path if prompted. -->
        <string>/opt/homebrew/opt/python@3.12/bin/python3.12</string>
        <!-- -u: unbuffered stdout/stderr. Without it, Python fully
             block-buffers output when it isn't attached to a real
             terminal (which a launchd job never is) — the process runs
             fine, but StandardOutPath/StandardErrorPath can sit empty
             for a long time before a buffer flush, making it look like
             nothing happened when it actually has. Confirmed live: the
             job was state=running the whole time even with empty logs. -->
        <string>-u</string>
        <string>/usr/local/bin/curant-facetime-answerer.py</string>
    </array>

    <!-- Loops forever polling for incoming calls, same shape as
         com.curant.watcher.plist, so it can run alongside the text
         watcher as a second, independent LaunchAgent. -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- launchd does not source .zshrc/.zprofile, so cliclick, ffmpeg,
         SwitchAudioSource (Homebrew), and curant-cli (installed to this
         Mac's personal ~/bin, confirmed via `which curant-cli`) won't be
         found on the default PATH unless it's set explicitly here.
         Without /Users/AbhyudayDhundhel/bin specifically, get_reply()'s
         subprocess.run(["curant-cli", ...]) fails outright with "No such
         file or directory" — confirmed live, every single reply on a
         real test call failed this way until this was added. -->
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/Users/AbhyudayDhundhel/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>

        <!-- BUG FIXED: this key was previously named CURANT_ACCESS_MODE,
             which curant-facetime-answerer.py has never actually read —
             see _read_call_access_mode()'s docstring: the real variable
             is CURANT_CALL_ACCESS_MODE, deliberately split from texting's
             CURANT_TEXT_ACCESS_MODE after a real incident where a SHARED
             key meant enabling open access for calls silently opened
             texting to anyone too. Confirmed live: with the old wrong
             key name, this plist silently had zero effect and the
             process fell back to the "approved" default regardless of
             what this said.

             'approved' (explicit here, matching the code's own default)
             does real caller-ID verification now — OCR-reads the caller
             ID off the incoming-call banner and only answers if it
             matches CUSTOMER_APPLE_ID/CUSTOMER_HANDLES, refusing (fails
             CLOSED, never answers unverified) if OCR isn't available or
             finds no match. Needs `brew install tesseract` and
             `pip3 install pytesseract` (with the break-system-packages flag, see SETUP_FACETIME_CALLS.md) — without
             those, every call gets refused rather than answered, which
             is the safe failure mode, not a bug. -->
        <key>CURANT_CALL_ACCESS_MODE</key>
        <string>approved</string>

        <!-- Real bug found live: bare BlackHole 16ch as the system output
             produces total digital silence for FaceTime call audio
             (RMS=0.0 across all 16 channels during a genuinely connected
             call, confirmed in a real test) , see the comment above
             SYSTEM_OUTPUT_DEVICE in curant-facetime-answerer.py. The fix
             is a macOS Multi-Output Device (Audio MIDI Setup, combining
             BlackHole 16ch with the Mac's real speakers), pointing
             system output at THAT instead of bare BlackHole 16ch while
             still capturing caller audio from BlackHole 16ch directly.
             "Curant Call Output" already exists on this Mac from earlier
             testing (confirmed live via SwitchAudioSource -a -t output)
             , this just wires it back in, since the script only uses it
             when this env var is explicitly set (defaults to bare
             BlackHole 16ch otherwise). -->
        <key>CURANT_FACETIME_SYSTEM_OUTPUT_DEVICE</key>
        <string>Curant Call Output</string>

        <!-- Dev/testing bypass, matching every other Curant launchd job
             this session (watcher, dailybriefing, proactive, weeklyrollup,
             meetingprep). curant-facetime-answerer.py's replies go through
             the same curant-cli relay path as texting, so without this
             every call would get the same not_activated failure until a
             real license is activated via curant-cli activate. -->
        <key>CURANT_DEV_UNLICENSED</key>
        <string>1</string>
    </dict>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/tmp/curant-facetime.log</string>

    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/tmp/curant-facetime-error.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
<!--
Install with:
  cp curant-facetime-answerer.py /usr/local/bin/curant-facetime-answerer.py
  chmod +x /usr/local/bin/curant-facetime-answerer.py
  cp com.curant.facetime.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
  launchctl load ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.curant.facetime.plist

This is a separate LaunchAgent from com.curant.watcher.plist (texting) —
loading both makes texting and FaceTime answering run at the same time,
independently. If one crashes or is unloaded, the other keeps running.

Check both are running:
  launchctl list | grep curant
  # expect to see BOTH app.curant.watcher and app.curant.facetime

Fallback coordinates, if you set CURANT_FACETIME_ACCEPT_XY as an env
var while testing in the foreground, need to be added to this plist's
EnvironmentVariables dict too — env vars exported in your shell are NOT
visible to a launchd job.

Logs land at /tmp/curant-facetime.log and /tmp/curant-facetime-error.log
while testing — move these somewhere more permanent once stable.

To stop just the FaceTime answerer without touching texting:
  launchctl unload ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.curant.facetime.plist
-->
